1
00:00:05,853 --> 00:00:07,702
Strike a bell and listen.

2
00:00:07,782 --> 00:00:10,025
The sound is bright and clear.

3
00:00:10,105 --> 00:00:12,539
And it is already passing as it sounds.

4
00:00:12,619 --> 00:00:15,152
At no point is there a sound that stays.

5
00:00:15,974 --> 00:00:18,095
Here is a koan to hold in mind.

6
00:00:18,175 --> 00:00:20,609
This moment ceases as it arises.

7
00:00:20,689 --> 00:00:23,317
Does it arise, or does it cease?

8
00:00:24,125 --> 00:00:28,243
This video is about how that question
is answered by two different ways of

9
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:31,789
thinking. And about why the
difference between them matters,

10
00:00:31,869 --> 00:00:35,041
not only for philosophy, but for practice.

11
00:00:35,841 --> 00:00:39,529
It draws on The Logic, a
section of After Anatta:

12
00:00:39,686 --> 00:00:45,204
notes by John Tan, known as Thusness, on
what turned out to matter after the anatta

13
00:00:45,284 --> 00:00:47,418
insight of 1997.

14
00:00:47,498 --> 00:00:50,652
The seven phases of insight
describe the seeing.

15
00:00:50,732 --> 00:00:53,197
These notes set out the logic behind it.

16
00:00:54,026 --> 00:00:56,745
Start with the claim
the author puts first.

17
00:00:56,825 --> 00:01:01,636
The logic of dependency cannot be
understood clearly by reasoning alone.

18
00:01:01,716 --> 00:01:05,617
It needs a direct insight into
what a phenomenon actually is.

19
00:01:05,697 --> 00:01:08,095
A sound, a thought, a cup.

20
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:11,466
The two logics can be learned as ideas.

21
00:01:11,546 --> 00:01:14,686
One starts with things, and
asks what happens to them.

22
00:01:14,766 --> 00:01:18,110
The other asks how anything is
picked out as a thing at all.

23
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:21,833
Explained like this, the difference
takes a minute to follow,

24
00:01:21,913 --> 00:01:23,740
and a minute to agree with.

25
00:01:24,562 --> 00:01:26,656
But agreeing changes very little.

26
00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:29,917
The logic of substance
is not a belief we hold.

27
00:01:29,997 --> 00:01:34,632
It is how things seem, before any
thought. The sound is simply there.

28
00:01:34,712 --> 00:01:37,417
The cup is simply there, on its own side.

29
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:42,539
As long as things are seen this way, even
the new logic is quietly read through the

30
00:01:42,619 --> 00:01:43,819
old one.

31
00:01:44,329 --> 00:01:47,578
So the author names three
insights after anatta.

32
00:01:47,658 --> 00:01:51,993
Without seeing them clearly, and
confirming them again and again in direct

33
00:01:52,073 --> 00:01:55,408
experience, the logic
stays a clever argument.

34
00:01:56,213 --> 00:02:02,192
The first. Every appearance is vivid, and
it cannot be found. Look at a red apple.

35
00:02:02,272 --> 00:02:06,119
The red is bright, impossible
to miss. Now look for it.

36
00:02:06,199 --> 00:02:09,203
Is it in the skin, in
the light, in the eye?

37
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:12,790
It is not found in any of
them, and not anywhere else.

38
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:14,611
Yet the apple is still red.

39
00:02:14,691 --> 00:02:17,695
Vivid and unfindable, at the same time.

40
00:02:17,775 --> 00:02:20,005
The tradition calls this emptiness.

41
00:02:20,085 --> 00:02:23,483
Not a void, and not a
ground behind the appearing.

42
00:02:23,563 --> 00:02:25,933
Only the appearing's not being found.

43
00:02:26,758 --> 00:02:31,298
The second. Appearance arises
whenever its conditions come together.

44
00:02:31,378 --> 00:02:36,583
Light, a surface, an eye,
attention: together, red appears.

45
00:02:36,663 --> 00:02:38,907
When one is missing, it does not.

46
00:02:38,987 --> 00:02:43,241
Nothing comes out of a hidden place,
and nothing goes back into one.

47
00:02:43,321 --> 00:02:46,302
The tradition calls this
dependent origination.

48
00:02:47,114 --> 00:02:51,722
The third. We habitually and
intuitively think inherently,

49
00:02:51,802 --> 00:02:54,969
and the way we do it follows
the shape of our language.

50
00:02:55,049 --> 00:02:57,646
A sentence wants a subject and a verb.

51
00:02:57,726 --> 00:03:02,849
So we say "lightning flashes", as if there
were a lightning apart from the flash.

52
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:05,023
But experience is never like that.

53
00:03:05,103 --> 00:03:07,061
The flashing is the lightning.

54
00:03:07,141 --> 00:03:09,779
The tradition calls this habit ignorance.

55
00:03:09,859 --> 00:03:14,779
Not a lack of information, but the innate
grasping at things as existing from their

56
00:03:14,859 --> 00:03:16,059
own side.

57
00:03:16,758 --> 00:03:20,523
The first two are one
fact, seen from two sides.

58
00:03:20,603 --> 00:03:24,083
What arises in dependence
is what is called emptiness.

59
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:26,411
The third is what hides that fact.

60
00:03:27,213 --> 00:03:29,836
And the third runs almost by default.

61
00:03:29,916 --> 00:03:32,880
Whatever experiences and insights we have,

62
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:35,312
it reshapes them before we notice.

63
00:03:35,392 --> 00:03:39,361
Many practitioners hold that
direct experience is what matters,

64
00:03:39,441 --> 00:03:41,888
and that view and logic come second.

65
00:03:41,968 --> 00:03:47,173
So practice becomes the removal of
concepts. Not thinking. Bare attention.

66
00:03:47,253 --> 00:03:48,577
Naked awareness.

67
00:03:49,406 --> 00:03:51,718
These practices do something real.

68
00:03:51,798 --> 00:03:56,949
With the commentary quiet, the clarity
of appearances can be tasted directly.

69
00:03:57,029 --> 00:03:58,742
But that is as far as they go.

70
00:03:58,822 --> 00:04:02,859
The habit of thinking inherently
does not need words to work.

71
00:04:02,939 --> 00:04:06,459
Silence the words, and it
carries on without comment.

72
00:04:06,539 --> 00:04:10,608
It reads the clear, wordless
experience in the old way.

73
00:04:11,429 --> 00:04:14,379
The author describes
three ways this happens.

74
00:04:14,459 --> 00:04:18,156
We taste luminous clarity,
and invent a witness:

75
00:04:18,236 --> 00:04:20,588
a knower standing behind the knowing.

76
00:04:20,668 --> 00:04:24,691
We meet the vividness of
appearances, and make it objective:

77
00:04:24,771 --> 00:04:27,558
a world out there, solid and given.

78
00:04:27,638 --> 00:04:31,077
We taste unfindability,
and make it private:

79
00:04:31,157 --> 00:04:33,174
all of it only in the mind.

80
00:04:34,033 --> 00:04:36,780
Three real tastes. Three inventions.

81
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:40,937
Each time the experience was
true, and the reading was false.

82
00:04:41,017 --> 00:04:44,959
And the three readings are the
great positions people argue about:

83
00:04:45,039 --> 00:04:47,636
consciousness as the ground of everything,

84
00:04:47,716 --> 00:04:52,106
a physical world simply there,
and a world that is only mind.

85
00:04:52,186 --> 00:04:54,212
The author passed through each of them.

86
00:04:54,292 --> 00:04:57,975
The way out of the last was
the logic of dependency itself.

87
00:04:58,055 --> 00:05:02,377
If a subject depends on its object,
what makes it truly a subject?

88
00:05:02,457 --> 00:05:06,512
An inside that depends on an
outside is not truly inside.

89
00:05:07,346 --> 00:05:10,717
The great masters said the
same, in their own ways.

90
00:05:10,797 --> 00:05:16,519
Atiśa wrote that calm abiding alone,
without the wisdom that sees emptiness,

91
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:18,897
does not end the obstructions.

92
00:05:18,977 --> 00:05:23,765
Tsongkhapa wrote that without the
wisdom that realises how things are,

93
00:05:23,895 --> 00:05:28,829
even renunciation and compassion
cannot cut existence at its root.

94
00:05:28,909 --> 00:05:31,750
Longchenpa advised that
when thoughts arise,

95
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:33,992
the direct view is crucial.

96
00:05:34,072 --> 00:05:39,671
And Dōgen, a Zen master, scolded those in
his day who thought the old Zen stories

97
00:05:39,751 --> 00:05:41,836
were beyond logical thought.

98
00:05:42,641 --> 00:05:47,348
Experience matters. But experience
without the view cannot do the work.

99
00:05:48,165 --> 00:05:49,824
Now return to the koan.

100
00:05:49,904 --> 00:05:52,337
This moment ceases as it arises.

101
00:05:52,417 --> 00:05:55,046
Does it arise, or does it cease?

102
00:05:55,849 --> 00:05:59,627
The logic of substance starts
with a thing: this moment.

103
00:05:59,707 --> 00:06:04,170
Arising and ceasing are what
happen to it. So it must choose.

104
00:06:04,973 --> 00:06:07,257
It arises? Then it is there.

105
00:06:07,337 --> 00:06:11,632
But it ceases as it arises, so
it is never there to have arisen.

106
00:06:11,712 --> 00:06:15,885
And if it stays even for an instant,
that instant has a beginning,

107
00:06:15,965 --> 00:06:18,932
a middle and an end, and it splits again.

108
00:06:19,737 --> 00:06:22,646
It ceases? Then something passes away.

109
00:06:22,726 --> 00:06:25,585
But what passes away, if it never stood?

110
00:06:26,402 --> 00:06:30,752
Both? Coming into being and
passing away are opposites.

111
00:06:30,832 --> 00:06:33,555
They cannot belong to
one thing at one time.

112
00:06:34,361 --> 00:06:36,794
Neither? Then nothing happens at all.

113
00:06:36,874 --> 00:06:39,136
Yet a sound is being heard right now.

114
00:06:39,957 --> 00:06:44,754
Every answer fails. Nāgārjuna
put the whole trap in one verse.

115
00:06:44,834 --> 00:06:50,275
There is no passing away either apart from
coming into being, or together with it.

116
00:06:51,073 --> 00:06:54,417
At this point the logic of
substance looks for a way out.

117
00:06:54,497 --> 00:06:57,229
A timeless Now that the
moments pass through.

118
00:06:57,309 --> 00:07:00,802
An awareness in which they
come and go. A pure flow.

119
00:07:00,882 --> 00:07:03,370
Each way out keeps the moment as a thing,

120
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:05,685
and only moves it somewhere else.

121
00:07:06,509 --> 00:07:09,866
The logic of dependency
starts one step earlier.

122
00:07:09,946 --> 00:07:13,969
It does not begin with a moment
that has arising and ceasing.

123
00:07:14,049 --> 00:07:16,850
It asks how "this moment"
is picked out at all.

124
00:07:16,930 --> 00:07:20,858
A moment is picked out together
with its arising and its ceasing.

125
00:07:20,938 --> 00:07:23,195
Arising, together with ceasing.

126
00:07:23,275 --> 00:07:27,153
None comes first, and none is
found apart from the others.

127
00:07:27,969 --> 00:07:32,304
In the author's words: the
moment doesn't arise nor cease,

128
00:07:32,384 --> 00:07:35,991
but is merely designated
as such upon conditions.

129
00:07:36,845 --> 00:07:40,271
Think of a staircase. Are
the stairs up, or down?

130
00:07:40,351 --> 00:07:42,037
In themselves, neither.

131
00:07:42,117 --> 00:07:45,855
They are up for someone climbing,
and down for someone coming down.

132
00:07:45,935 --> 00:07:49,659
That depends on where the walker
stands, and which way they face.

133
00:07:49,739 --> 00:07:54,673
And the stairs are really climbed, and
really descended. The moment is the same.

134
00:07:54,753 --> 00:07:59,007
It is called arising from one
side, and ceasing from the other.

135
00:07:59,087 --> 00:08:02,381
It is not nothing, and it
is not only in the mind.

136
00:08:03,185 --> 00:08:08,186
So "ceases as it arises" is not a
paradox. It is a plain description.

137
00:08:08,266 --> 00:08:13,798
There is no "it" standing between the
arising and the ceasing, to do either.

138
00:08:13,878 --> 00:08:16,859
The question is not
answered. It falls away.

139
00:08:17,674 --> 00:08:22,354
The eight steps of The Logic each
come back to this koan from one side.

140
00:08:23,174 --> 00:08:26,111
The first sets the two
logics side by side:

141
00:08:26,191 --> 00:08:30,341
one starts with things, the other
asks how anything is picked out.

142
00:08:31,170 --> 00:08:33,821
The second asks what exactly is denied.

143
00:08:33,901 --> 00:08:37,150
Only the "it", the moment
standing on its own.

144
00:08:37,230 --> 00:08:41,090
Deny too little, and a
timeless Now takes its place.

145
00:08:41,170 --> 00:08:44,111
Deny too much, and nothing happens at all.

146
00:08:44,926 --> 00:08:49,833
The third goes looking for the moment, the
way the old masters looked for a chariot.

147
00:08:49,913 --> 00:08:55,077
Is it the arising? The ceasing?
Both? Their owner? Their container?

148
00:08:55,157 --> 00:08:57,156
It is not found in any of them.

149
00:08:57,236 --> 00:09:00,218
Yet moments are named, counted and timed.

150
00:09:01,034 --> 00:09:04,664
The fourth gives the koan two
answers that do not conflict.

151
00:09:04,744 --> 00:09:09,120
Conventionally, the bell starts
and stops, and we can say when.

152
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:14,446
Looked for, nothing that arises or
ceases is found. Not two worlds.

153
00:09:14,526 --> 00:09:16,611
One moment, met two ways.

154
00:09:17,430 --> 00:09:22,273
The fifth shows why all four answers
fall together. Each one assumed an "it".

155
00:09:23,098 --> 00:09:25,355
The sixth names the two traps.

156
00:09:25,435 --> 00:09:30,247
Say "it arises", and the mind finds
somewhere for the moment to last:

157
00:09:30,327 --> 00:09:32,040
the eternalist trap.

158
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:37,937
Say "it ceases", and the mind decides
that nothing matters: the nihilist trap.

159
00:09:38,017 --> 00:09:39,273
Both keep the "it".

160
00:09:40,118 --> 00:09:41,995
The seventh answers the worry.

161
00:09:42,075 --> 00:09:45,419
If no moment is found,
how does anything work?

162
00:09:45,499 --> 00:09:50,310
A moment with its own fixed nature
could never arise or cease at all.

163
00:09:50,390 --> 00:09:55,545
It is because nothing stands on its own
that things can arise, change and work.

164
00:09:56,357 --> 00:09:59,538
And the eighth gathers it
all into four questions,

165
00:09:59,618 --> 00:10:01,195
ready for any question.

166
00:10:01,275 --> 00:10:02,989
What would a substance view say?

167
00:10:03,069 --> 00:10:05,135
What does dependency say instead?

168
00:10:05,215 --> 00:10:08,469
What is affirmed conventionally?
What is never found?

169
00:10:09,286 --> 00:10:13,228
There is one more turn. We think
our way of solving is logical.

170
00:10:13,308 --> 00:10:16,231
We bring questions like
"does the self exist?"

171
00:10:16,311 --> 00:10:19,655
or "what happens when I
die?", and try to answer them.

172
00:10:19,735 --> 00:10:23,649
But each question already assumes
a thing with its own nature.

173
00:10:23,729 --> 00:10:28,717
Answered in the same frame, any answer
only swaps one thing for another.

174
00:10:28,797 --> 00:10:30,633
The view offers something else.

175
00:10:30,713 --> 00:10:33,925
Not a better answer, but a
way to question the question.

176
00:10:34,741 --> 00:10:39,375
Nothing is lost when the "it" is
not found. The bell is still heard.

177
00:10:39,455 --> 00:10:41,087
The clock still keeps time.

178
00:10:41,167 --> 00:10:42,976
Actions still bear fruit.

179
00:10:43,056 --> 00:10:46,468
Mutual dependence has
no first and no higher.

180
00:10:46,548 --> 00:10:50,897
No substrate underneath, and
no power hidden inside things.

181
00:10:50,977 --> 00:10:55,394
Dependent arising is not a lesser
layer over something more real.

182
00:10:55,474 --> 00:10:58,456
There is nothing more real
underneath for it to cover.

183
00:10:59,273 --> 00:11:04,822
Listen to the bell once more. It is vivid.
It is gone. And nothing went anywhere.

184
00:11:05,637 --> 00:11:09,103
Ask what arises, and
the koan is a paradox.

185
00:11:09,183 --> 00:11:14,135
See that no "it" is found, and
the koan is simply this moment.

